(1) DONE Add a node similar to autoinject that injects random numbers (in the
"value" field). The minimum/maximum values should be configurable together with
the distribution function.

(2) DONE Add a filter to pass the messages through except setting/adding the
configured properties. The configuration dialog should make it possible to add
arbitrary property/value pairs, similar way the Filter node settings supports a
dynamic list of widgets.

(3) Let us design a way to pass a two dimensional array of numbers in the
message instead of one single numerical value. Maybe creating a new message
class would be best, then the nodes could decide if they support one, the other
or both message types?

(4) DONE Let us create a headless cli tool to execute the saved worksheets.
This program should not be able to save worksheet, just execute them and so the
execution of certain tasks can be run from the command line. There should be a
non-mandatory command line option to make the program gracefully exit after a
pre-set time elapsed.

(5) DONE In the node popup menu the "Configure" menu item should be the last
shown after a menuitem separator.

(6) DONE Add a new property to the nodes called "worksheet" so that later on we
can add support for multiple worksheets similar way it is done in spreadsheets.
This should not go into the settings dialog and the default value should be
"Worksheet" for backward compatibility.

(7) DONE The "Save" toolbar button and the "Save" menu item should only be enabled
if the loaded worksheet is changed.

(10) DONE Let us add a lot more crypto currencies to the Rate node.

(11) DONE Let us download icons for the known crpyto currencies from the web.
Let us add them to the Rate node settings comboboxes.

(12) Let us implement node measuring certain processes something like this
ssh eth4 "pidstat -p 5365,6902 30 1 2>/dev/null | awk '/Average/ {print}'"
     Average:      UID       PID    %usr %system  %guest   %wait    %CPU   CPU  Command
     Average:     1000      5365  101.63   11.07    0.00    0.00  112.70     -  geth-pulse
     Average:     1000      6902  155.43   14.63    0.00    0.00  170.07     -  geth-pulse

but the PID should be obtained by checking the pre-set process name(s).

(13) Add a node to periodically check if a process exists on a remote host,
report success if so, false if not. Report the PID as "value".

(14) DONE Currently the functional tests having problems when an other instance of
the pipnode runs because all instance tries to register for the same DBus name
and so the functional tests can not contact the started program. Let us add a
command line option to set the DBus name when pipnode is started and let's
update all the functional tests to use a custom DBus name when starting the
program.

(15) The functional tests are executed very fast so that the user can't see what
happens. Let us slow them down a little but. They can finish fast, but every
step that causes visual update should take about 1 second at least.

(16) DONE The HTTPS client should have an input. The idea was that we send
messages to the HTTPS client and it will contact the server and send the
messages to them. Then the server will produce the messages on the output.

(17) DONE The https server now have two empty entries showing the certificate
and private key that suggests to the user that the server will not work without
these. The pipnode should provide these by default so the node works out of the
box. Maybe these should be autogenerated at the install time, maybe there
should be a "generate" button. Better to have it generated at install, then
the user does not have to open the node configuration at all.

(18) DONE Let us create an mqtt source. The default settings should be:
tcp://mqtt.homelab.local:1883

(19) Add an MQTT sink node that takes incoming messages and publishes them to
the configured broker. Same connection properties as the source (url,
username, password, client-id, TLS) QoS and retain
flag. The published payload should be data.payload when present (serialised
back to JSON when it is an object/array, or stringified when it is a
scalar), falling back to a JSON dump of the whole data bag. This is the
counterpart to the source the same way HTTPS Client is the counterpart to
HTTPS Server. Also, we need to be able to get the topic from the incoming
message, removing the leading /mqtt, ignoring the message if its topic does not
start with /mqtt/.

(20) Add an on-demand poll mechanism for Tasmota devices that piggybacks on
the new MQTT sink: a node (or a worksheet recipe documented in the help)
that publishes "cmnd/tasmotas/Status 5" to the broker and listens on
"stat/+/STATUS5" through an MQTT source, then surfaces each device's
IPAddress / Hostname / Mac from the StatusNET reply. The result is a
discovery feed that can be wired into the HTTPS Client (or a future plain
HTTP client) to drive per-device REST calls without any external scan.

(21) Fix the homelab WiFi/router configuration so the IoT VLAN is reachable
from the main LAN. Current state: this workstation is on 192.168.0.0/16 via
br0 (gateway 192.168.0.1) and the Tasmota fleet is on 10.1.1.0/24 — the
broker at 192.168.0.5 sees both, which is why MQTT works, but there is no
inter-VLAN route from 192.168.0.0/16 to 10.1.1.0/24 so direct IP access
(HTTP to a device's web UI, ping, the IPs returned by STATUS5) fails. A
phone connected to the IoT WiFi can reach the devices but has no internet
either, which matches the captive-isolated-guest profile the WiFi router
was deliberately configured for. Decide between (a) leaving it isolated
and routing all device access through MQTT (the TODO #20 path — cleaner,
no router changes), or (b) adding inter-VLAN routing + a NAT/firewall rule
on the WiFi router so 192.168.0.0/16 can reach 10.1.1.0/24 for HTTP. If
(b), document the router-side config (static route, ACL exception) here
so the change is reproducible the next time the firmware is reset.

(22) Implement a tasmota concentrator. Here is how a discovery message can be
sent to a tasmota device:
mosquitto_pub -h mqtt.homelab.local -p 1883 -t 'cmnd/sonoff19/Status' -m '5'

(23) Split libpipnode into a headless "core" library and a GTK "gui" library
so pipnode-run can be installed and run on servers without pulling the GTK
toolkit (gtk+-3.0, gtksourceview-4, webkit2gtk). The runtime is already
headless-clean: src/pn-run.c, lib/pn-flow.c, lib/pn-node-factory.c and ~35 of
the 71 lib sources make zero GTK/cairo calls. The two blockers are build-time:
(a) one libpipnode.la compiles every source with GTK_CFLAGS and links the full
toolkit, and both binaries link it; (b) lib/pn-node.h #includes <gtk/gtk.h> and
bakes GdkRGBA + cairo_t*/GtkWidget*/GtkNotebook* into the PnNodeClass vtable, so
even pure-logic nodes drag in GTK at compile time. ~15 "dual-nature" nodes
(Rate, LED, Knob, Dial, Analog-Meter, Graph, Switch, Table, Chat...) mix
receive() logic + cairo drawing (and sometimes a custom GTK dialog tab) in one
.c file, so the split line is not a clean per-file cut. Refactor pn-node.h to
forward-declare the GTK/cairo types used only in vfunc signatures so the class
ABI (and plugin ABI) stays stable. Prerequisite: build the characterization
tests first (a number of pipnode-run tests that lock down current headless
behavior) so the refactor can be proven not to change behavior. Open decisions
to settle before starting: (1) core dependency strictness -- pragmatic (drop
the GTK toolkit but keep cairo/pango for offscreen drawing) vs strict (zero
GTK/GDK/cairo, replace GdkRGBA with our own color type+parser); (2) whether
visual nodes' logic must remain loadable headless or they become GUI-only;
(3) whether to re-tier the bundled plugins (network/shell/tasmota -> core only)
in the same pass.
STATUS: DONE. The lib is split into a GTK-free libpipnode-core + libpipnode-gui;
all 24 dual-nature nodes are split (logic -> core, drawing/dialog -> gui);
pipnode-run links core only and is verified GTK-free (objdump DT_NEEDED). The
plugin ABI (now v3) gained a companion GUI module (pn_plugin_gui_init in
<plugin>-gui.so) so a plugin ships GTK-free logic the server installs + bespoke
GUI the editor alone loads. A declarative GTK-free settings schema
(PnSettingsSchema, stored via per-type qdata so no ABI change) lets most nodes
customise their dialog with no GTK at all; the imperative build_* vfuncs remain
the escape hatch and always win. All bundled plugins re-tiered: network/tasmota/
image are core-only single .so; shell + sound-effects are two-tier (logic .so +
companion -gui.so). Docs updated (PLUGINS schema/companion model + server-
installable checklist; README "Running headless"; INSTALL "Runtime tiers and
packaging" with the pipnode-core vs pipnode-gui distribution split). Decisions
settled: (1) strict zero-graphics core (glib/gio/json-glib/libsoup/gmodule +
gdk-pixbuf only); (2) visual nodes' logic stays headless-loadable; (3) bundled
plugins re-tiered; no --disable-gui build switch (D5). DROPPED as out of scope
(decided 2026-05-23): the list-of-records schema editor kind (Filter/Set would
keep their imperative dialogs -- their logic is already headless) and full-GUI
plugin nodes. The temporary HEADLESS-SPLIT-PLAN.md has been removed now that the
work is complete.

(24) DONE (follow-up complete): the pure parsers/aggregators of the IO
nodes are now exported non-static and unit-tested on canned fixtures --
Temp (pn_temp_parse_remote_lines / pn_temp_aggregate), Http
(pn_http_split_body_and_status), Weather (pn_weather_parse_current /
pn_weather_code_description, extracted from emit_message), Tts
(pn_tts_derive_voice_label / pn_tts_voice_index_for); Net-IO was already
done (pn_net_io_parse_net_dev). See tests/unit/test-pn-{temp,http,weather,
tts}.c. The per-second delta/rate math stays in the trigger (entangled
with the monotonic clock + prev-sample state). Original note follows.

PnAutoTrigger now has a synchronous test seam: a construct-only
"autostart" property (default TRUE; pass FALSE to skip the worker thread) and
pn_auto_trigger_run_once_sync(), which runs the trigger() vfunc once on the
calling thread and makes pn_auto_trigger_emit_on_main() deliver synchronously,
so a test can capture the emitted message without a main loop (see
tests/unit/test-pn-auto-trigger.c). Follow-up: export the pure
parsers/aggregators currently buried as static helpers in the auto-trigger IO
nodes (Temp parse_remote_lines/aggregate, Http, Tts, Weather, the rest of
Net-IO) -- declare them non-static in their headers, per the pn-disk-io /
pn-net-io precedent -- and add full-node tests driven by run_once_sync. Ties
into #23 (the headless/GTK split): the same nodes are the ones whose logic
should remain loadable and testable headless.

(25) DONE Run an XFCE panel applet's worksheet without the editor as a
throwaway process, and without hosting the flow inside xfce4-panel itself (a
node/plugin crash must never take down the panel). The worksheet now runs in a
background "engine": pipnode-editor started as a D-Bus service
(--gapplication-service, auto-activated via data/dbus-1/services/
org.pipas.pipnode.service). Each worksheet lives in a panel-backed PnWindow
(pn_window_new_panel) that is built but kept hidden -- its flow ticks off the
main loop; "Edit" just gtk_window_present()s it; closing autosaves and hides
rather than destroying, so the flow survives. The engine exposes
org.pipas.pipnode.Engine (RunWorksheet/GetDisplayValue/SetInput/PresentEditor/
CloseWorksheet + the ValueChanged signal, keyed by worksheet path) alongside the
existing test-only org.pipas.pipnode.Worksheet interface. Two new core nodes
bridge the flow to the panel: Panel Display (PnPanelDisplay, a sink whose last
value the applet shows) and Panel Input (PnPanelInput, a source a click drives).
The applet (panel-plugins/pipnode-xfce-applet) is now a thin GDBus client linking
NO pipnode library. Tests: tests/test_panel_engine_run.py + unit tests
test-pn-panel-{display,input}.c. The headless CLI (pipnode-run) stays the
server tool; the engine is the desktop-session runtime.

Uniqueness: only the engine (--gapplication-service) and test instances
(--dbus-name) take the org.pipas.pipnode name as a single addressable instance;
a normal `pipnode-editor [--file X]` launch is G_APPLICATION_NON_UNIQUE (decided
in main.c::argv_wants_unique), so it runs as its own process and never forwards
the file to the engine.

(26) The Text to Speech node should default to "any engine you find" + "any
